# Linked windowTick harnesses

`event-converter-test`, `host-entry-test`, and `render-boundary-test` are
local-only regression checks. Run them from the repository root through the
local build guard, after building the core tests:

```sh
TCBUILD=/path/to/tcbuild.sh
"$TCBUILD" build_all --core-tests-only
"$TCBUILD" sh -ec '
  core/tests/windowTick/event-converter-test
  core/tests/windowTick/host-entry-test
  core/tests/windowTick/render-boundary-test
'
```

They use the generated compiler/link metadata in
`core/tests/allCoreTests/build-linux` and the core library in `core/build-linux`.
The render harness resolves archives relative to that link metadata and builds
the `tcxImGui` target there if its required archive is missing. Build, compile,
link, and assertion failures return a nonzero status.

These Linux checks need a C++20 compiler and the normal TrussC development
libraries, plus EGL and Mesa software rendering. They need no display server:
the render harness uses surfaceless EGL, and the other two use deterministic
native fixtures. Their copied macOS and Windows code runs against recording
structs rather than those operating systems.

Together they take about 2 minutes, over the CI budget, so they stay
local-only. CI runs only the offline fixture boundary checks below.

The fixture generators list their stub and header dependencies explicitly.
An unknown `_sapp_tc_*` helper fails generation with its name; copy its
production body or add a deliberate replacement to the appropriate list.
The dependency checker has offline regression checks:

```sh
python3 tools/tests/test_window_tick_harnesses.py -v
```
